hey need from secure external collaboration, workspaces to e-signature processes, and content workflows improving employee productivity and accelerating business processes. It offers web, mobile, and desktop applications of its solutions on a platform, as well as the ability to develop custom applications. The company has a partnership with OpenAI to reimagine AI- and content-powered enterprise work that allows users to securely browse, access, and work with Box content directly in ChatGPT. The company has a partnership with NVIDIA Corporation that enables organizations to securely execute AI-powered workflows at scale. The company was formerly known as Box.net, Inc. and changed its name to Box, Inc. in November 2011. Box, Inc. was incorporated in 2005 and is headquartered in Redwood City, California.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Aaron  Levie | Co-Founder, CEO & Director | Age 40 | Compensation: $257,076 (FY2026)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 2K people. Revenue per employee of $404K ranks in the above the sector median among Technology peers, with revenue growing 15%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $120K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $80K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (4/10).","financial_summary":["Box, Inc. is a mid-cap technology business with a $6.4B market cap on $1.2B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +6% over the past three years and +15% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size. Operating margins are expanding by +7.3pp year-over-year, which is one of the strongest signals that the moat is deepening.","Returns on capital run at 115% against a 8% cost of capital \u2014 a 107-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat. Gross margins of 72% confirm genuine pricing power.","The business throws off $350M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 30 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 3.09\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 2.4\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and interest coverage runs at 7.8\u00d7.","Shareholder yield of 4.1% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and stock-based comp at 20% of revenue is silent dilution an owner has to account for, and insiders hold 3.3% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.3\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 102% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 3 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":1.0151625664287984},{"text":"Very elevated short interest (16.4% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $4.3M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Technology sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 9% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Technology ETF trading well below its long-term trend","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Elevated sector volatility (52% annualized) adds uncertainty","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Sector under pressure with a 18.0% decline over the past 6 months",